{
  "image_name": "3afdd480c2c15f33920ff5836ee5de44.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how tumor metabolism is shaped by different tissue microenvironments. In the general tumor setting, cancer cells sit in a gradient of blood perfusion and exchange nutrients with surrounding stromal and immune cells. Labels indicate competition or transfer of glucose, asparagine, lactate, and fibroblast-derived metabolites such as fatty acids, amino acids, and pyruvate, emphasizing that nutrient availability varies across the tumor.\n\nThe other panels show this idea in specific organs. In pancreatic cancer, tumor cells appear to rely on nearby activated stellate cells, with arrows highlighting autophagy-related support and the supply of compounds including nonessential amino acids and lipid species such as LPC/LPA. In the liver, the drawing maps tumor growth onto normal liver zonation: periportal and pericentral hepatocyte regions have different metabolic programs, and the tumor-associated area is linked to infiltrating immune cells, HMGB1, and reduced liver functions such as the urea cycle and albumin production. In bone marrow, tumor cells interact with bone and adipose niches; adipocytes release fatty acids and seem to undergo lipolysis, while exosomes from tumor cells may stimulate this process. Overall, the figure shows tumors adapting to, and exploiting, organ-specific metabolic ecosystems.",
